WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520
Speaker 1: So many of you will remember that&nbsp;
I've been giving good news about declining COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.920
cases on the show in the United States, dating&nbsp;
back to really the first few days of September.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:18.720
I've also been saying since late October that&nbsp;
decline is starting to slow down. And then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.800
got to well, the cases are now flat. They're no&nbsp;
longer declining. We don't know if this is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.600
inflection point and cases will start going back&nbsp;
up or if they'll stay flat or if they'll continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:37.600
on down. We don't know what will happen heading&nbsp;
into winter. We now have a modest but clear trend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.880
which is that cases in the United States are going&nbsp;
back up. And in fact, they've been on the upswing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.720
since roughly Halloween, so close to a couple of&nbsp;
weeks and cases are now up in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:58.000
just shy of about 10 percent per day.&nbsp;
We had gotten down to 71000 cases a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.360
which is crazy to think that, wow, what if we&nbsp;
had a huge decline down to 71000 cases a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:12.800
that is now back up to 77000 cases a day? And this&nbsp;
is really, really not good. Now, if we were at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:21.040
point where cases no longer meant deaths, either&nbsp;
because enough vaccination has taken place where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.040
most folks are protected against serious illness&nbsp;
and death, or because of a less deadly strain that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.880
has evolved, this wouldn't be as bad, but those&nbsp;
things haven't happened yet. And so far, deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.440
have been following cases by about three to four&nbsp;
weeks. And just as we saw cases stop their decline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:45.600
three to four weeks ago, deaths are now flat.&nbsp;
Deaths are no longer declining. They're stuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:52.160
at about 1100 COVID deaths per day in the&nbsp;
United States. So there is every possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.640
that we are at the beginning of a new spike and&nbsp;
a potentially very ugly winter. Now, maybe not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:05.680
Maybe not. We don't really know yet, but the case&nbsp;
increase has started and the death decline has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.960
stopped. Now, often, as we've seen throughout&nbsp;
the pandemic, what happens in the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.200
follows what happens in other parts of the world.&nbsp;
Part of the reason for that is that many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.920
spikes are caused by variants, which we first&nbsp;
observe in other parts of the world, like Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.160
So far, the global numbers are not looking&nbsp;
particularly auspicious for the United States over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.840
the next three months. Cases around the world are&nbsp;
up even more than in the United States. Globally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:38.320
cases had bottomed back. They'd bottomed out&nbsp;
at four hundred and three thousand cases a day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.240
Globally, cases are now up to four hundred seventy&nbsp;
two thousand cases a day. So while in the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:50.160
cases are up nine or 10 percent, globally, cases&nbsp;
are up about 17 percent. That's not a great signal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:57.840
for what is to come here as far as global deaths.&nbsp;
Those are slightly up. Global deaths had bottomed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:05.360
out around 60 600 a day. Deaths are now back up&nbsp;
to 100 a day around the world. That's an increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:12.720
of about six or seven percent. So if there is&nbsp;
any good news to point to and even this, you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:19.920
see why it's not even that great we can point&nbsp;
to hey, vaccinations every day are now climbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.040
in the United States. They're not incredible&nbsp;
numbers. Looking at the graph on the screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.560
if you're watching today, you might see that daily&nbsp;
vaccinations really bottomed out in mid-July.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.840
And then as Delta surged and discussion of Delta&nbsp;
surged, vaccinations also started to increase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:43.120
In late July, we were down to about 550000 doses a&nbsp;
day that has climbed and climbed, almost tripling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:49.120
And we're now doing over the last week about 1.3&nbsp;
million doses per day. Unfortunately, and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:57.120
just horrible. The not so good thing is that the&nbsp;
increased doses per day are significantly driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.920
by people who already have two doses getting a&nbsp;
third dose. So you're not vaccinating that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.280
new people. If you ask the question how many&nbsp;
new people are getting vaccinated each day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.560
it might only be a couple hundred thousand to&nbsp;
maybe 300000, sadly. I mean, it's good that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.440
are getting third doses and more protection,&nbsp;
but a huge portion of the daily doses today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:26.400
Are people getting a third dose good for those&nbsp;
people? Not so good for the population at large.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.680
Now that five to 11 year olds can start to get&nbsp;
vaccinated in the U.S., we do expect the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.520
of kids with one dose in that age group to be&nbsp;
at roughly a million. Right now, about a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:43.360
kids have already gotten vaccinated. That's good,&nbsp;
but the misinformation continues to kill people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:51.520
We have a report from Colorado from CBS Denver,&nbsp;
which says that according to a Colorado ICU nurse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:57.200
there are many COVID patients there in the ICU&nbsp;
with COVID. They don't believe the virus is real.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:04.480
They believe the hospital made them sick, and&nbsp;
in some cases they called the nurses murderers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:10.560
So that's very bad. We will see what things&nbsp;
look like by Thanksgiving. I've started to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.760
questions from from some of you about what is my&nbsp;
Thanksgiving plan from the standpoint of medical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.160
from the standpoint of COVID. I'm going to be&nbsp;
doing a testing Thanksgiving. So it's going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:26.400
maybe outdoor, maybe indoor, depending on the&nbsp;
weather. Everybody will be tested and I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.600
interviewed, everybody will be vaccinated and&nbsp;
tested. When we interviewed Vincent Racaniello&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:37.360
from Columbia University and I said to him,&nbsp;
Sir, is this foolproof if people get a test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:42.240
right as they're going in? Is there any way&nbsp;
you might be incubating and contagious but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:48.160
negative? And he basically said if you're&nbsp;
using the test for a few hours gathering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:55.680
it's essentially foolproof in the sense that this&nbsp;
was what he said. If you were incubating the virus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:02.480
and you don't yet have symptoms and you test&nbsp;
negative, you're almost certainly not contagious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:08.320
for the next few hours. So what you wouldn't want&nbsp;
to do because of this possibility that you've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.920
infected, you're incubating, but you don't yet&nbsp;
test positive. Don't use the test as justification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:19.200
to hang out for a week without doing any more&nbsp;
testing, because then you might turn positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:24.000
and turn contagious. But if you're doing a&nbsp;
few hours gathering, Vincent Racaniello was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.640
confident that if everybody has a negative&nbsp;
test, everybody's going to be not contagious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:33.840
at least for a few hours. So that's my plan for&nbsp;
Thanksgiving. I'm curious to hear about yours

